      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fordsville was named for early merchant Elisha Ford in 1830, honoring his role in the town’s founding.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fordsville%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Founded around 1833 as Ohio County’s second‑oldest town, Fordsville grew from 1790 settlers like the Mobberleys and Huffs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fordsville_kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Louisville Railroad Depot, built in 1916 with Prairie School influences, now houses the Fordsville Historical Society Museum.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
